That work is licensed **CC BY 4.0**
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). This plugin is an adaptation
of it, and the following changes were made:

  • Restructured from a linear document into five task-scoped skills plus
    reference catalogues loaded on demand.
  • Generalised from the playbook's assumed many-parallel-trials study service
    to an explicit parallelism-regime switch, with the playbook's own
    documented fallbacks used in the low-parallelism regime, and with a
    local/remote execution split the source does not address.
  • Every ported claim was assigned a grade on this plugin's evidence ladder,
    and sections the playbook's authors marked as open research questions
    (the 🤖 marker) carry an explicit source-uncertainty flag.
  • Merged with the tier catalogues carried forward from `parml`, whose
    speed↔quality pricing the playbook does not use, and cross-referenced by
    item id.
  • Material outside the playbook's scope was added — architecture and
    numerics (tier D), an optimiser reference, and a pinned literature index.

  No endorsement by the playbook's authors is implied, and this is not an
  officially supported Google product.

================================================================================
2. Carried forward from `parml` (paretoml)
================================================================================

No source files were copied from any project. The tier catalogues are original
prose. Where an entry is graded `measured-elsewhere`, its measurement comes from
a cited source; the two that supplied the largest share are:

  • KellerJordan/cifar10-airbench (MIT, © 2024 Keller Jordan) and its paper,
    "94% on CIFAR-10 in 3.29 Seconds on a Single GPU" (arXiv:2404.00498) —
    the add-to-baseline / remove-from-final ablation protocol, the additivity
    finding, the frozen-input-transform, near-identity-init, parameter-group
    learning-rate, derandomised-augmentation, iterate-averaging and multi-view
    inference results, the run-count-for-significance arithmetic, and the
    measured calibration cost of test-time augmentation.

  • hiverge/cifar10-speedrun (MIT) — the batched-optimiser, vectorised
    augmentation, compiled-step, confidence-gated inference and
    per-change wall-clock attribution results.

Both are MIT-licensed. Original copyright notices remain with their respective
authors. This derivative retains attribution as required by the MIT License. No
endorsement by the original authors is implied.

Two corrections to the above sources are original findings, re-derived
numerically:

  • The steady-state Nesterov step amplification is 1/(1−m), not 1 + 1/(1−m)
    (see `references/tier-a-algorithmic.md`, A10).
  • A magnitude-target schedule that is immediately followed by an unconditional
    renormalisation is inert — measured swing ≈ 1×10⁻⁴ relative across a whole
    run (see `references/pitfalls.md`, P2).

================================================================================
3. Other cited sources
================================================================================

Every other claim that rests on a citable source names it at point of use and
resolves through `references/literature.md`, which pins each entry to a DOI or
arXiv identifier. No text was copied from any of them.

The protocol tier deliberately shares vocabulary with the `ar` (autoresearch)
plugin in this same marketplace — noise floor, locked harness, keep threshold —
so that a `/tml:plan` handoff into `/ar:start` needs no translation.
